<!--
    Application-wide look. Merged from App.xaml rather than the window, because
    a dozen dialogs are built in code as bare Windows and inherit application
    resources only — styling the window would leave them on the stock 2006 look.

    Everything here is presentation. No style sets Margin, Width, Height or
    alignment, so nothing moves: spacing still comes from the Padding and Margin
    the layout already specifies. Sizes that were tuned by hand stay tuned.

    ComboBox is retemplated, popup and all. It kept the stock template while the
    app was light-only; the stock one draws its own chrome from system brushes,
    so it stayed light when the palette went dark.
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:shell="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Shell;assembly=PresentationFramework"
                    xmlns:ui="clr-namespace:SimplifiedUi">

    <!-- The palette lives in its own dictionary, merged ahead of this one by
         App.xaml. Every style below names colours only through those keys, so
         swapping light for dark is one line there and nothing here changes.

         Those references are DynamicResource, not Static: a static one is
         resolved once when the style loads and keeps the brush it found, so
         swapping the palette at runtime would leave every existing control
         wearing the old theme. Dynamic costs a lookup per use and buys a toggle
         that works without restarting. -->

    <!-- ─────────────── Brand ───────────────
         The wordmark itself is the one thing here that is not shared: each app
         has its own, and each keeps it in a Brand.xaml merged after this
         dictionary. Everything that draws it - the caption bar, its colours,
         its sizing - is below, and is the same everywhere. -->

    <!-- ─────────────── Icons ───────────────
         Google's Material Icons, taken from their SVG sources so the shapes are
         the real thing rather than something approximated. The F1 prefix selects
         SVG's nonzero fill rule, which is what keeps the cut-outs — the hole in
         the gear, the slot in the floppy — as holes instead of filled.

         Each icon's own 24x24 bounding-box path is dropped: it carries
         fill="none" and exists only to pad the artwork, and as geometry it would
         make every icon a filled square. A Viewbox scales them, so the 24-unit
         coordinates never need touching. -->

    <!-- ─────────────── The wordmark ───────────────
         The word "simplified", as path geometry converted from the SVG, so it
         stays sharp at any DPI and takes whatever colour it is given. Studio
         and ERP draw their own two-line lockups; everything else pairs this
         with its own name beside it.

         Drawn at 111.95 x 23.39, so a Viewbox with only a Height set gives it
         the right width on its own. -->
